If demos recolor background cells between same-colored markers, avoid assuming that every horizontal and vertical alignment is independently interpolated. That rule can accidentally fill numerous cells where alignments cross, overlap, or belong to distinct fragments. Transcribe the changed-cell set in every demonstration, then test whether fills belong to a coherent outline/object or to selected segments; record aligned gaps left untouched as negative evidence. Replay the complete rule, including all unchanged cells, before applying it to a test.

A wrong global-interpolation submission falsifies that complete hypothesis. Reassess object structure and marker roles instead of repeating the same strategy with a changed color or a narrower guessed subset. Spend remaining demo budget only when a specific candidate distinction would change the predicted test grid.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/structured-outline-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review", "file": "references/large-output-analysis.md", "content": "# Large-output analysis

When output dimensions greatly exceed input dimensions, establish the transformation structurally rather than assuming a conventional same-size edit. First test whether the output consists of exact input tiles. Otherwise, check whether the input is preserved as a corner block and compare every added region against the input and against other added regions for a reproducible generation rule. Validate the whole output, including all added cells; extensive additions must not be dismissed as noise. A single example may establish dimensions without establishing how the additions are generated. Request another demo only when stated competing constructions predict different test outputs and the added example can discriminate between them.
